{"seq":1,"at":"2026-09-17","kind":"imported","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0479"},"reason":"Article of the Quiztopia bank (written for card c040): researched and transcribed in September 2026, converted to the quiz platform on 2026-10-20.","by":"Quiztopia import","ref":"quiz-archive/transcripts/quiztopia/c040.json"},
{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0479"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge everyday/food","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/everyday__food.g1.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",593],["+"," Long before anyone distilled them, agaves gave Mexico an older drink: pulque, the milky, slightly sour fermented sap of the maguey agave. Rock drawings from around AD 200 already show rituals connected with it, and the Aztecs reserved it largely for priests, the elderly and festivals."],["=",208],["+"," Across the Atlantic the cereal changes. America's native whiskey must be distilled from a mash of at least 51 per cent maize and aged in new charred oak barrels, and its name, Bourbon, is borrowed from the French royal dynasty, the House of Bourbon – probably by way of Bourbon County, organised in Virginia's Kentucky district in 1785 and named in gratitude for French help in the War of Independence."],["=",407],["+","Bourbon | maize | grass (cereal) |\n| "],["=",33],["+","\n| Pulque (fermented, not distilled) | maguey sap | asparagus family (succulent) |"],["=",5],["-","a"],["+","succulents,"],["=",1],["-","succulent, two "],["=",25]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",585],["+"," Lange bevor jemand sie destillierte, schenkten die Agaven Mexiko ein älteres Getränk: Pulque, den milchigen, leicht säuerlichen vergorenen Saft der Maguey-Agave. Schon Felszeichnungen aus der Zeit um 200 n. Chr. zeigen Rituale, die mit ihm verbunden sind, und die Azteken behielten ihn weitgehend Priestern, Alten und Festen vor."],["=",200],["+"," Jenseits des Atlantiks wechselt das Getreide. Amerikas eigener Whiskey muss aus einer Maische mit mindestens 51 Prozent Mais gebrannt und in neuen, innen ausgeflammten Eichenfässern gereift werden, und sein Name, Bourbon, ist dem französischen Herrschergeschlecht der Bourbonen entlehnt – vermutlich über das Bourbon County, das 1785 im Kentucky-Distrikt Virginias gegründet und aus Dankbarkeit für die französische Hilfe im Unabhängigkeitskrieg so benannt wurde."],["=",385],["+","Bourbon | Mais | Süßgras (Getreide) |\n| "],["=",27],["+","\n| Pulque (vergoren, nicht destilliert) | Saft der Maguey-Agave | Spargelgewächse (Sukkulente) |"],["=",5],["-","eine"],["+","Sukkulenten,"],["=",1],["-","Sukkulente, zwei "],["=",25]]}]},
